WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.509
Decimal places extend the same base-ten system to parts smaller than one

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:15.300
Three tenths occupy thirty hundredths, and seven more hundredths bring the shaded total to thirty seven hundredths, written as zero point three seven.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.017
A trailing zero changes the written precision, not the amount

00:00:21.017 --> 00:00:25.433
Four tenths and forty hundredths shade exactly the same region

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:32.500
To compare decimals, compare corresponding place values instead of treating their digit strings as whole numbers.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.178
When adding decimals, align their units

00:00:36.178 --> 00:00:43.707
One point two becomes one point two zero so that tenths line up with tenths and hundredths with hundredths

00:00:43.707 --> 00:00:48.067
The increase of thirty five hundredths reaches one point five five.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:53.747
For multiplication, think in fractions rather than shifting a point mechanically

00:00:53.747 --> 00:00:56.932
Three tenths times four tenths is twelve hundredths

00:00:56.932 --> 00:01:04.100
The product is smaller than either factor because we are taking only part of an amount below one.
